kim","vote":"no"},{"name":"Nita Green","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10950","person_slug":"nita-green","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Pauline Hanson","party":"One Nation","person_id":"tvfy_10280","person_slug":"pauline-hanson","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Penny Allman-Payne","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11002","person_slug":"penny-allman-payne","vote":"no"},{"name":"Peter Whish-Wilson","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10780","person_slug":"peter-whish-wilson","vote":"no"},{"name":"Raff Ciccone","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10915","person_slug":"raff-ciccone","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Richard Dowling","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11056","person_slug":"richard-dowling","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Sarah Hanson-Young","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10711","person_slug":"sarah-hanson-young","vote":"no"},{"name":"Steph Hodgins-May","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_11020","person_slug":"steph-hodgins-may","vote":"no"},{"name":"Tyron Whitten","party":"One Nation","person_id":"tvfy_11060","person_slug":"tyron-whitten","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Varun Ghosh","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_11017","person_slug":"varun-ghosh","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Wendy Askew","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10914","person_slug":"wendy-askew","vote":"aye"}],"name":"Business — Consideration of Legislation","noes":13,"question":"David Shoebridge I seek leave to move a motion relating to consideration of the Home Affairs Legislation Amendment (2025 Measures No. 1) Bill 2025 as circulated. Leave not granted. Pursuant to contingent notice standing in the name of Senator Waters, I move: That so much of the standing orders be suspended as would prevent me moving a motion to provide for the consideration of a matter, namely a motion to give precedence to a motion relating to a consideration of the Home Affairs Legislation Amendment (2025 Measures No. 1) Bill 2025. The government and the coalition have agreed to ram through the Home Affairs Legislation Amendment (2025 Measures No. 1) Bill 2025 which is a bill to strip natural justice, a fundamental right, from asylum seekers and whomever else the government chooses to deport to Nauru. The government has run the argument that it needs to be rammed through this week because it is urgent. I can tell you we had a three-hour committee hearing in relation to this bill last night. Officials from Home Affairs came and they provided their case for urgency, and it was demolished—utterly demolished. I'll give credit to Senator Cash, who picked apart the argument for urgency that was presented by Home Affairs. In questioning from Senator Cash, Senator David Pocock and me, what Home Affairs eventually said was, 'Well, we'd like this legislation because we want to make sure that our current unfair processes, which we're going to continue regardless, cannot be legally challenged.' They also said that, regardless of whether the bill goes forward, they're going to continue to grind away and their actions will not change whether or not the bill passes. Then, when tested about whether there was any particular case that they desperately needed this legislation for, Home Affairs basically said no and that the cases that are either in the criminal courts or in the High Court are going to grind through and that they didn't need this legislation for any urgent matter dea","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-9772","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2025-09-04/1"}
